
Neither, I think — the case dissolves once you look at what determines the slot rather than how it renders.

## The artefact often *determines* the objective

`summarize this report` does state an end state: a summary of the report exists. That isn't an absent objective needing a soft form; it's a **present** one, available by transcription. D.9's own criterion is "determines under a single reading," and it does.

So these cases never reach the rendering question. Objective stays a definition slot, `open:` stays bounded at four, no special case, no third render form:

```
objective:  a summary of the attached report exists
```

Your instinct was right and my reversal was aimed at the wrong target. What I was actually worried about is a narrower class than "objective missing."

## The discriminator: does the artefact name entail a completion test?

```
summarize this report          → "covers the attached report"     ✓
write an SOP for month-end     → "month-end close fully described" ✓
build an AI financial advisor  → ?                                 ✗
improve our onboarding         → ?                                 ✗
```

Where a completion test exists, objective is determined and populated. Where the name is a *category* or a *capability* rather than a bounded thing, no test is available and objective opens.

To keep this from becoming a judgment call at generation time — the same failure mode as generated example banks — one hard constraint makes it auditable:

```
D.9a  OBJECTIVE FROM ARTEFACT

  Objective MAY be populated as the existence of a named artefact
  where a completion test is available from the input alone.

  The populated value MUST introduce no noun absent from the input.
  If stating the end state requires a noun the user did not supply,
  the objective is not determined → "not established" → open:.
```

`a summary of the attached report exists` — *summary*, *report*, both present. Passes.
`an FCA-compliant tool that helps users plan for retirement` — *retirement* absent. Fails, opens. Which is correct: that was my inference about what the advisor is for, and it's exactly the invention this whole review has been closing off.

## Your four examples, honestly

The test splits them, and I don't want to overclaim:

| input | result |
|---|---|
| `summarize this report` | populated — subject present |
| `write an SOP for closing month-end books` | populated |
| `write an SOP` | opens — SOP for *what* |
| `create a dashboard` | opens — dashboard of *what* |
| `build an FAQ page about our returns policy` | populated |

So two of your four are fragments missing a **subject**, not requests with no purpose. Bare `create a dashboard` does warrant a question — but note what question. Under the old framing it would have been "what should be true when this is done?", which invites a confabulated purpose (*"so stakeholders can make informed decisions"*). Under D.9a the gap is the artefact's subject, and the question asks for that. Narrower, answerable, no invented telos.

Where I fully concede: for a self-bounding artefact, demanding a separable purpose produces noise, and noise in `objective` is worse than noise elsewhere because it's the field a consumer optimises against. That was the real cost of my reversal and it's larger than the cost I was avoiding.

## Why the residual variation lands elsewhere

The obvious objection to artefact-as-objective: a summary for a board differs from a summary for a lawyer, so surely purpose matters?

It does, and the partition already catches it — that difference is **audience** and **register**, both of which remain live and can open independently. Purpose-shaped variation distributes across the other slots rather than needing a home in `objective`. Which is a point in favour of the partition generally: `objective` carries the end state, not the rationale for it.

## `[N/A]` cap

```
D.11a  [N/A] reasons: ≤12 words, single clause, no conjunction.
       Must name the property of the goal that makes the slot
       inapplicable. Over-length → truncate at review, not at render.
```

My own worked example broke this — *"form is software; register is fixed at interface design, not at goal level"* is 13 words and two clauses. Corrected:

```
register:  [N/A] — fixed at interface design, not goal level
```

Shorter is also more legible, which is the point.

## Net effect on the FCA example

Unchanged. `build an AI financial advisor` introduces no completion test and any end state I could write would import absent nouns, so objective still opens:

```
objective:    not established
audience:     not established
...
open: objective, audience
```

But the question it generates is now better scoped — it asks what the advisor must accomplish for a user, rather than fishing for a purpose behind the artefact.

## State

Architecture closed, on my read: partition enumerated, `open:` bounded at four, D.9a resolving the objective case without a special render, `[N/A]` capped, banks drafted.

Two open items, both yours rather than mine:

1. **Confirm the definition-slot list** against the live spec — `open:`'s bound depends on it being exactly those four under whatever names it uses.
2. **Owner for echo-rate sampling**, or drop the threshold from the spec and rely on the out-of-domain rule alone. A metric with no owner reads as a guarantee and isn't one.

Say the word and I'll produce the full consolidated spec text with all of this folded in.
